NAME
       nc - connect to a port and send it text (Cyber Range's simulated ncat)

SYNOPSIS
       nc [-v] HOST PORT
       nc -z [-v] HOST PORT

DESCRIPTION
       nc ("netcat") opens a plain connection to a port on a computer and
       sends it whatever you type or pipe in, then prints what comes back.
       Many services simply read a line of text and answer with a line, so nc
       is a quick way to talk to them by hand.

       The usual way is to pipe a line in:

           echo "hello" | nc localhost 30000

       Without a pipe, nc reads one line from your keyboard, sends it and
       prints the answer.

       In Cert Lab and Cyber Range nc is simulated: it only reaches the pretend
       machines and services of the range.

OPTIONS
       -v     Say what happened (connected, refused...).

       -z     Only check whether the port is open; send nothing.

EXIT STATUS
       0 when the connection was made, 1 when it was refused or timed out.

EXAMPLES
           nc -zv localhost 30000
           cat password.txt | nc localhost 30000

SEE ALSO
       nmap(1), curl(1), ss(8)
